<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas Arvis [Laws of the Evening Prayer]<br>Based on <span class="lr">LM I:49</span> (<i>LaShemesh Ohel Bahem</i>)<br>§§ 82–96, plus <i>Rashay Perakim</i><br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Continuation of <i>Tefilas HaMinchah</i> Halachah 7</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 82 — Yaakov Established <i>Tefilas Arvis</i>: Prayer Even in Total Darkness</div>

<div class="p">The three <span class="h">Avos</span> revealed His G-dliness and <span class="h">Malchus</span> in the world — each through prayer. Avraham established <span class="h">Shacharis</span>: the dawn-light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>. But Adam failed to contract it — he gazed where he had no permission (Tikunai Zohar), flawed his thought, damaged his prayer. Even after his <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, and even through subsequent <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (Noach, Mesushelach, Shem, Eiver), none worked to reveal His <span class="h">Malchus</span> to the world until Avraham — the first to repair the contractions, revealing G-dliness on earth (Rashi: through him He became <span class="v">"G-d of the earth"</span>). Avraham began the tikun of prayer (the essential revelation of <span class="h">Malchus</span>). Yitzchak added <span class="h">Minchah</span>: when the <span class="h">mochin</span> withdraw, only a <span class="h">reshimu</span> remains — prayer for strengthening at the time <span class="h">da'as</span> departs. But Yaakov, choicest of the <span class="h">Avos</span>, added <span class="h">Arvis</span>: after the sun has set entirely, in the darkness of night (= total withdrawal of <span class="h">mochin</span>). He repaired the tikun of prayer even then — so we can reveal His <span class="h">Malchus</span> even from utter darkness. <span class="v">"Even darkness is not dark to You; night shines like day."</span></div>

<div class="p">Yaakov's bed was complete — he fathered all of Israel. But he foresaw through <span class="h">ruach hakodesh</span> that they would sin. He therefore strengthened and repaired <span class="h">Arvis</span>: that even one who fell into total darkness (= the prolonged exile = <span class="v">"darkness upon the face of the deep"</span> = <span class="v">"He placed me in darkness"</span>) could draw the tikun of prayer (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = revelation of <span class="h">Malchus</span>). He perceived that the root of all corruption derives from excessive light → Breaking of Vessels → absence of <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. Each person must contract the light and guard from bad thoughts. Yaakov merited tikun of thought (= tikun of the <span class="h">Bris</span>) more than all the <span class="h">Avos</span>: his bed was complete (<span class="h">raishis ono</span>). From his immense holiness he could descend to the depths of every Jew's fall in every generation until <span class="h">ikvesa d'Meshicha</span>.</div>

<div class="p">As Rabbainu taught: the greater and holier the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the lower he can descend to work on their tikun. Yaakov specifically repaired <span class="h">Arvis</span> — in the time of total darkness. Even then: <span class="mx">"no good will is ever lost."</span> Every movement, every good point one detaches from bad to good — the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yaakov) gathers it into the holy structure. No matter what happens between the good points — strengthen to snatch more. The <span class="h">tzadik</span> raises the <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> from the lowest places: the lower the source, the more precious the arousal. Through this, Yaakov builds the Third <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (which will never be destroyed) — through Israel returning to Hashem by the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 83 — The <i>Bais Midrash</i> = the Third <i>Bais HaMikdash</i>, Built in Yaakov's Merit</div>

<div class="p">All tikun through the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>/<span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (= the <span class="h">Batai K'nesiyos</span> and <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span> of Israel = <span class="h">mikdash m'at</span>). The essential: the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> named after the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> of each generation (§§68-69). The Midrash (Eichah): <span class="v">"every great house"</span> = the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> of R' Yochanan ben Zakkai (called "great" — encompassing all the holiness of 481 <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span> in Yerushalayim). Through the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span>'s holiness: every good point snatched even from utter darkness is gathered by the <span class="h">tzadik</span> into the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>/<span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> he builds constantly. Through this: one becomes part of the nation, included in the holy gathering — building the Third <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (in Yaakov's merit, never to be destroyed). Our Sages: the first two were in Avraham and Yitzchak's merit; the third in Yaakov's. Yaakov repaired <span class="h">Arvis</span> when he "encountered the place" (<span class="v">"this is none other than the house of G-d"</span>) — tikun of the Third House: prayer even from darkness (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = <span class="h">Malchus</span> revealed).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 84 — <i>Is'arusa d'l'sata</i>: "If Hashem Wanted to Serve Himself, He Wouldn't Need You"</div>

<div class="p">All tikun through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. Hashem desires that we specifically serve Him from this world. As Rabbainu said to his followers: <span class="mx">"if Hashem wanted to serve Himself, He wouldn't need you."</span> Yet it is hard — the evil inclination expands, especially against those entering <span class="h">avodas Hashem</span> (<span class="mx">"he stretched his hand against the great"</span>). Necessarily: help from above — yet all is from Him (<span class="v">"who preceded Me that I should repay?"</span>). Still, some arousal from below is needed. This is unknowable: <span class="mx">"the wonders of the Perfect in knowledge."</span> Therefore every arousal is exceedingly precious. <span class="mx">"Open for Me like a needle's eye."</span> Immediately upon arousal: <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span> follows — "whoever comes to purify is assisted." This is the essence of the <span class="h">yichudim</span> made through prayer: uniting <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> with <span class="h">d'l'ala</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is what Yaakov perceived when he "encountered the place" (= the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>). Our Sages: why didn't the place stop him when he passed by? Because he hadn't yet given his mind to pray — and Heaven won't stop a man without his own arousal. Only when he resolved to return did <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span> respond: the place leapt toward him. Then he perceived the awesome wonder of <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>: even from the deepest, most distant places, every arousal is precious. Therefore he established <span class="h">Arvis</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 85 — "He Encountered the Place": Yaakov's Ladder; the Third <i>Bais HaMikdash</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"He encountered the place and lodged there, for the sun had set."</span> The "place" leapt toward him — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (the Third Building that will endure). He perceived the bitterness of the prolonged exile (= <span class="v">"he lodged there for the sun had set"</span> = the darkness of exile in body and soul). He established <span class="h">Arvis</span>: understanding the preciousness of <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> even from the depths of darkness. <span class="v">"He took from the stones of the place and set them at his head"</span> — the fallen sparks and souls (<span class="v">"the holy stones are poured out at every street-corner"</span>), raised and bound in his holy thought (<span class="mx">"all is clarified through thought"</span>). <span class="v">"He lay down in that place"</span> — he descended to the lowest places to clarify and raise souls. <span class="v">"He dreamed: a ladder set on the earth, its top reaching heaven"</span> — man must ascend from level to level like a ladder. All for tikun through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. <span class="v">"Angels of G-d ascending"</span> = <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>; <span class="v">"descending"</span> = <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span> in response. Through every holy arousal from below, angels are created → arousal from above descends to help. Through their unification: all <span class="h">yichudim</span> and tikun.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Yaakov awoke... 'Surely Hashem is in this place, and I did not know.'"</span> Even Yaakov, in his immense holiness, did not initially perceive this place's holiness — until his own arousal. This he implanted in all Jewish souls for all generations: even those who think Hashem is not in their place should know and believe He is there (<span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His <i>kavod</i>"</span> — only hidden). If one arouses from below, one will find Him. <span class="v">"He feared: 'how awesome is this place — this is none other than the house of G-d.'"</span> He said <span class="v">"<i>ain zeh</i> [this is not]"</span> = hinting at the destruction of the first two houses. But the third — <span class="v">"the house of G-d"</span> — still requires many tikun through <span class="h">Batai K'nesiyos</span> and <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span> (= <span class="h">mikdash m'at</span>) until <span class="v">"the house of G-d"</span> is built and all return to Hashem. <span class="h">Amain</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 86 — "Give Strength to G-d": <i>Is'arusa d'l'sata</i> and <i>d'l'ala</i> Through the <i>Mikdash</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Give strength to G-d... the G-d of Israel gives strength and power to the nation."</span> Rabbainu (<span class="lr">LM I:147</span>): first one must strengthen in holy boldness — <span class="v">"give strength to G-d"</span> = <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> (Israel adds power on high: <span class="v">"through G-d we shall do valiantly"</span>). Through this: <span class="v">"G-d gives strength to the nation"</span> = <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span>. All through the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s holiness (= the <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span> = <span class="h">mikdash m'at</span>): <span class="v">"awesome is G-d from Your sanctuaries."</span> = <span class="v">"how awesome is this place."</span> The <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> and <span class="h">d'l'ala</span> unite in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (= <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span> — "the Tent of Meeting": <span class="v">"I shall meet you there"</span>; <span class="v">"I am my beloved's and my beloved is mine"</span>). The essential: the future <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> that the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> build in every generation through their holy gathering — placing in every heart (even the worst) the arousal to return, gathering every good point, raising <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, until <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span> descends.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 87 — "G-d Seats the Solitary in a House": Unifying the Arousals; Even Prisoners Are Freed</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"G-d seats the solitary in a house"</span> = the <span class="h">yichudim</span> made through Israel's service (uniting <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> with <span class="h">d'l'ala</span>). Specifically "in a house" — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (= the <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span>). <span class="v">"He brings out prisoners into prosperity [<i>b'kosharos</i>]"</span> — even those imprisoned by their sins (<span class="v">"dwellers of darkness and the shadow of death, prisoners of affliction and iron"</span>) are brought out to <span class="h">kashrus</span> [fitness], becoming proper people. <span class="mx">"The <i>Batai K'nesiyos</i> and <i>Batai Midrashos</i> purify all"</span> (our Sages on <span class="v">"how goodly are your tents, Yaakov"</span> — like a stream that purifies). All this: for one who truly desires and strives to draw close to Hashem and to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. But one who strays entirely: <span class="v">"only the rebellious dwell in a parched land."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 88 — Even the Nations Can Draw Close Through the <i>Bais HaMikdash</i></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Haftarah</span> of a public fast concludes: <span class="v">"the foreigners who join Hashem... I shall bring them to My holy mountain and gladden them in My house of prayer... for My house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations."</span> Even non-Jews can draw close through the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s holiness (= a house of prayer). <span class="v">"So says Hashem, who gathers the dispersed of Israel: I shall yet gather upon him, upon his gathered ones."</span> Every soul gathered strengthens the <span class="h">Bais Tefilah</span> — more are drawn in turn: <span class="v">"I shall yet gather upon him."</span> The <span class="h">Bais Tefilah</span> is built through gleaning souls and good points — and the more gathered, the stronger it becomes.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 89 — <i>Tefilas Arvis Reshus</i>: Yaakov's Tikun for the Depths of Exile</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Tefilas Arvis Reshus</span> [the evening prayer is optional]: Yaakov established the strengthening to pray even from the depths of darkness (= the bitter exile in body and soul) — building the Third House (in his merit). What did Yaakov tikun if <span class="h">Arvis</span> is optional — surely one is not forbidden to pray at night! Rather: Yaakov foresaw the bitter expansion of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> upon everyone. In the darkness: one is in the state of lost speech (= <span class="v">"mute dove"</span>; <span class="v">"I was mute, I did not open my mouth"</span>; <span class="v">"I was mute, silent — I held back from good"</span> — said about the exile). Without the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s power, all would despair of prayer. This is Yaakov's tikun: through the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> from his seed, even in the darkest night, a person will awaken with strong desires to pray and return. As Rabbainu warned: <span class="mx">"just do not despair of crying and prayer"</span> — [in Yiddish: <span class="mx">"as long as one does not give up from crying — one cries, one pleads..."</span>] — then everything turns to good.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Arvis Reshus</span> = Yaakov's holiness implanted in every Jew (from his seed) is the point of <span class="h">emes la'amiso</span> [truth in its truest form] = <span class="h">ratzon</span> [will] = <span class="h">ohr hapanim</span> [light of the face]. <span class="v">"Grant truth to Yaakov"</span>; <span class="v">"seekers of Your face — Yaakov, Selah."</span> Even now, in the bitterness of exile (= <span class="h">ikvesa d'Meshicha</span>): each person should look toward the ultimate truth, strengthening with strong desires and yearnings: <span class="v">"I remembered Your name at night"</span>; <span class="v">"I recall my song at night — with my heart I converse, my spirit searches"</span>; <span class="v">"on my bed at night I sought the one my soul loves."</span> Even when it seems one cannot find — <span class="v">"I sought but did not find"</span> — yet: <span class="v">"I shall arise and go about the city."</span> This is the tikun: in exile's darkness, searching with strong desires until speech bursts forth: <span class="v">"I was mute, silent... my heart burned within me — in my meditation a fire blazed — I spoke with my tongue."</span> This is the essential counsel for prayer and <span class="h">hisbodidus</span> — through good desires and yearnings. <span class="mx">"Do not make your prayer fixed"</span> — yet a fixed obligation is necessary for the masses. But Yaakov descended to the very end (<span class="h">ikvesa d'Meshicha</span>) — <span class="h">Arvis Reshus</span>: that even in utter darkness, one should not despair but strengthen with desires until one awakens voluntarily to pray. This is the true tikun of prayer.</div>

<div class="p">Yaakov is named for the <span class="h">akeiv</span> [heel] and end (= <span class="h">ikvesa d'Meshicha</span>; <span class="v">"his hand grasped Esav's heel"</span>). Souls fallen to the heel — most souls of these generations. In truth they are exceedingly lofty souls that fell greatly (Sefer HaGilgulim). Yaakov grasped Esav's heel to not let him conquer these souls — raising them from heel to head: <span class="v">"Yisra'el"</span> = <span class="h">li rosh</span> [I have a head] = descent for the ultimate ascent. Yet the exile is prolonged — tikun requires <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. Therefore Yaakov labored to produce the complete bed of Israel — from which <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who are sparks of <span class="h">Mashiach</span> emerge in every generation, instilling the strength to not despair even in <span class="h">ikvesa d'Meshicha</span>. This is <span class="h">Arvis Reshus</span>: even optional — it is a great tikun (without it, all would despair).</div>


<div class="sn">§ 90 — "How Goodly Are Your Tents, Yaakov": the <i>Batai Midrashos</i> as Garden by a River</div>

<div class="p">All through the holy gathering the <span class="h">tzadik</span> assembles (= the <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> = <span class="h">mikdash m'at</span> = the Third <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> in Yaakov's merit). <span class="v">"How goodly are your tents, Yaakov — your dwelling places, Israel"</span> — the <span class="h">Batai K'nesiyos</span> and <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span>. Bilam saw that Israel's strength through all the exiles (especially this long final one) is through these. Bilam blessed Israel three times (= three buildings of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>). Bilam and Balak were great sorcerers who foresaw the destructions and wished to uproot Israel entirely. But Hashem turned the curse to blessing. After the first blessing, Balak was undeterred (foreseeing the first House's destruction); after the second, still (foreseeing the second's destruction). At the third, Hashem forced Bilam's mouth to open with: <span class="v">"how goodly are your tents, Yaakov"</span> — through the holy tents and dwelling-places (= <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span> of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>), Israel cannot be swallowed. <span class="v">"Like streams stretched out"</span> — our Sages: as a stream raises from impurity to purity, so the <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Like a garden by a river"</span> — a garden beside a river needs no rain; the water flows to it immediately upon planting. So too: in the <span class="h">Batai K'nesiyos</span> and <span class="h">Batai Midrashos</span>, all prayers and Torah and service done within the holy gathering — <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span> descends immediately (from the river flowing from Eden to water the garden). In the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s holy gathering: the <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span> is close and ready, immediately supporting — unlike other "gardens" where one must wait for rain.</div>

<div class="p">The prophet strengthens Israel to return (the <span class="h">Haftarah</span> of <span class="v">"Seek Hashem"</span>): <span class="v">"let the wicked abandon his way... return to Hashem"</span>; <span class="v">"as the earth brings forth its growth, as a garden causes its plantings to sprout, so Hashem will cause..."</span> — His abundant mercy will cause <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'ala</span> to sprout immediately. <span class="v">"As the rain and snow descend from heaven..."</span> The essential: some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> (<span class="v">"seek Hashem while He may be found"</span>) — especially through <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span> (our Sages on that verse). All through the <span class="h">Bais Tefilah</span> (= <span class="h">Bais Midrash</span> = <span class="h">mikdash m'at</span>). <span class="v">"Like tents Hashem has planted"</span> — like precious spices. = <span class="v">"my spikenard gave its fragrance"</span> (said about the Calf — Hashem already prepared the remedy: <span class="mx">"it gave — not abandoned"</span>). As Rabbainu explained (<span class="lr">LM II:8</span>, <span class="h">Tik'u</span>): the <span class="h">tzadik</span> can rebuke with such a voice that sins transform to merits, ascending as good fragrance. <span class="v">"Like cedars by water"</span> = the <span class="h">tzadik</span> called a cedar: <span class="v">"the <i>tzadik</i> shall flourish like a palm, grow tall like a cedar in Lebanon."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 91 — <i>Bereishis</i> = <i>Bais Raishis</i>: the House Built by Purifying Thought</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Bereishis"</span> = <span class="h">Bais Raishis</span> [house of beginning]. <span class="h">Raishis</span> = purifying thought (= <span class="h">Chochmah</span> in the brain = <span class="v">"the beginning of wisdom"</span>). The world (resembling a house [our Sages]) was created for purifying thought: overpowering good thoughts against bad (= the essential inclinations). Through this: the holy house is built (= <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>) — good thoughts gathered from amid bad ones (= the essential <span class="h">birur</span> [clarification]: <span class="mx">"all is clarified through thought"</span>). = The teaching above: <span class="h">Bereishis</span> = two <span class="h">Raishis</span> = Nissan and Tishrai. In Nissan: the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> was completed and dedicated. In Tishrai: the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. Both are essential holy houses encompassing <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">tata'ah</span> — built and sustained through overpowering good thoughts against bad (= <span class="h">Raishis</span>). Nissan and Tishrai = days of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. In <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>: all gather to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>; then the <span class="h">shofar</span> = gathering the dispersed: <span class="v">"on that day the great <i>shofar</i> shall be sounded, and those lost... and dispersed... shall bow to Hashem on the holy mountain in Yerushalayim."</span> Nissan = head of the festivals (all festivals commemorate the Exodus). On festivals: commanded to ascend to the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (= gathering). Therefore: <span class="h">Bereishis</span> = <span class="h">Bais Raishis</span> (for this heaven and earth were created). = <span class="v">"With wisdom a house is built"</span> = through <span class="h">Chochmah</span> in the brain = purifying thought.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 92 — "Let Your House Be a Meeting Place for the Wise"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"Let your house be a meeting place for the wise"</span> — where the true sages gather (= the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright who overpower good thoughts against bad: only they are truly called wise). Only there should your house be. <span class="mx">"Dust yourself in the dust of their feet"</span> — break many obstacles and struggle in the dust of their feet to draw close, through obstacles and suffering. One cannot approach holiness without breaking many obstacles: <span class="v">"it was good for me to be afflicted, that I might learn Your statutes."</span> The essential tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> for all: through the meeting place of the wise (= the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s holiness).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 93 — Marriage: Drawing a Soul from Above All Worlds; the Seven <i>Betlers</i></div>

<div class="p">[This teaching (<span class="lr">LM I:49</span>) was said by Rabbainu at his daughter's wedding.] The <span class="h">chasan</span> [groom] is called up to the Torah on the Shabbos before the wedding — with many accompanying him in joy, honored with honey confections and nuts. The union of <span class="h">chasan</span> and <span class="h">kallah</span> [bride] at its supernal root is exceedingly lofty — above all four worlds (as <span class="lr">LM I:17</span>: <span class="mx">"I am a light creature in the sea"</span>). The light of enthusiasm in a Jewish heart = <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, rooted in the soul's source. <span class="v">"He breathed into his nostrils a living soul"</span> — from His own breath. But one must contract the enthusiasm for the world's existence (= the qualities). After contraction: judgment takes hold (= the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>'s root). The tikun only through man with free will. Adam failed immediately. Since then: immense clarifications are needed. All tikun through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of each generation (the Seven Shepherds). The completion through <span class="h">Mashiach</span> (encompassing all) = <span class="h">Mashiach ben David</span> (= <span class="h">Malchus</span> = <span class="v">"the sweet singer of Israel"</span>). Rabbainu revealed <span class="h">Mashiach</span>'s greatness somewhat in the Tale of the Seven <span class="h">Betlers</span> [beggars]: each blessed the children with their gift. The first (the Blind Man) told of memory — the smallest boasted he remembered what happened when his umbilical cord was cut. Each boasted higher. But the Blind Man himself boasted: <span class="mx">"I remember nothing at all"</span> — and he was the greatest of all.</div>

<div class="p">All this = <span class="h">chasunah</span> [marriage] — for the sake of Jewish children, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> (<span class="v">"in the multitude of people is the King's glory"</span>). But <span class="h">Malchus</span> cannot be drawn without many contractions — in each, from highest to lowest, one must contract in holy wisdom to prevent the <span class="h">klippos</span>' hold. The lower the world, the harder the battle. In <span class="h">Atzilus</span>: only the slightest hold; in <span class="h">Asiyah</span> (physical bodies): their hold is tangible. Yet: <span class="v">"you shall rule over it"</span> (Rashi: if you wish, you can overpower). Few win completely; even great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> did not escape some subtle error. The completion through <span class="h">Mashiach</span> who conquered the battle entirely — boasting <span class="mx">"I remember nothing at all"</span> = passed through every level from before <span class="h">Atzilus</span> to birth in this world, mastering all. Through him: every Jewish <span class="h">chasunah</span> done in holiness (with <span class="h">chuppah</span>, <span class="h">kidushin</span>, <span class="h">berachos</span>, a <span class="h">minyan</span>, and joy) draws the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power — sweetening all contractions and judgments at every level — revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> through this union. <span class="v">"What He forbade us, He permitted us"</span> — in the <span class="h">berachos</span> of <span class="h">airusin</span>: <span class="v">"He forbade the betrothed and permitted the married."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 94 — "<i>Asi MiLevanon Kallah</i>": From the Highest to the Lowest, the <i>Tzadik</i> Raises All</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Come from Levanon, bride — come from Levanon."</span> Rashi: from the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s destruction to exile and back. All alludes to our matter: from the supernal <span class="h">Lavan</span> [white] through all levels (where the <span class="h">klippos</span> take hold = exiles) until returning to the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s holiness. <span class="v">"You shall come, gaze from the peak of <i>Amanah</i> [faith]"</span> — the tikun through <span class="h">emunah</span> in Hashem and His servants (the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of each generation). <span class="v">"From the peak of Snir and Chermon, from the dens of lions, from the mountains of leopards"</span> — even from there, one ascends through <span class="h">emunah</span>. <span class="v">"You have captivated me with one of your eyes, with one link of your necklace"</span> — He treasures every good point found in Israel despite all their corruption. = The teaching of <span class="v">"<i>Azamrah</i>"</span> (<span class="lr">LM I:282</span>). Through the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who passed through every level and stood firm: he can raise all from the bottom to the top through holy <span class="h">emunah</span>, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. All = <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span> — said on the verse <span class="v">"he is like a <i>chasan</i> [groom] emerging from his canopy."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 95 — the <i>Chasan</i> Ascends to Torah; Honey and Nuts; the Israel/<i>Egoz</i> Parable</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">chasan</span> "ascends" to Torah (= <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>). Through the Shabbos Torah reading (seven <span class="h">olim</span> = Seven Shepherds repairing the seven days of building = all qualities): one receives through the <span class="h">tzadik</span> an illumination from the Torah's root (= <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span> = Shabbos = <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = <span class="v">"Hashem's Torah is complete, restoring the soul"</span>). From there: the tikun of <span class="h">chasunah</span> (= revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span>). Many accompany him (<span class="v">"in the multitude of people is the King's glory"</span>; <span class="v">"Your people are all <i>tzadikim</i>"</span>). Joy begins: arousing the <span class="h">shir pashut</span> etc. Custom to distribute honey confections: <span class="v">"more desirable than gold... sweeter than honey"</span> (adjacent to <span class="v">"Hashem's Torah is complete"</span>). Honey = the fierce judgments that attach to this world's sweetness (especially the primary desire — <span class="h">d'vash</span> = <span class="h">ishah</span> numerically). <span class="v">"The lips of the strange woman drip honey... her end is bitter as wormwood"</span> (= Shimshon's error). Even in holiness: <span class="v">"if you find honey, eat your fill"</span> = even enthusiasm for Hashem must be contracted (= Ben Azzai and Ben Zoma's error: <span class="v">"they found honey"</span>). But now, at a holy <span class="h">chasunah</span>: the honey is sweetened through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power — judgments in the honey are sweetened.</div>

<div class="p">Nuts (<span class="h">egozim</span>): <span class="v">"I descended to the garden of nuts."</span> Rashi: why are Israel compared to a nut? The shell is hard but inside is fine food — even sinners of Israel are full of <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. Also: even if thrown in mud, the inside remains unspoiled. Despite all the filth of exile and cravings — the inner holiness is not ruined (the hard shell protects the fruit). In the end: the mud is washed off, the shell cracked, and the pure food extracted — all through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 96 — Seven Wedding Blessings = Seven Shepherds = Seven <i>Betlers</i></div>

<div class="p">The seven <span class="h">berachos</span> of <span class="h">chasan</span> and <span class="h">kallah</span> = drawing holiness from the Seven Shepherds (= the Seven <span class="h">Betlers</span>). From there: the holiness of Israel's birth, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. = The seven <span class="h">olim</span> on Shabbos. Through the <span class="h">chasan</span>'s <span class="h">aliyah</span> to Torah on the Shabbos before the <span class="h">nisuin</span>: he receives the power for the Seven <span class="h">Berachos</span>.</div>

<div class="ssn"><i>Rashay Perakim</i> [Chapter Headings] for <i>Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah</i>, on the Torah "<i>LaShemesh</i>":</div>

<div class="p">At first He intended to create the world with strict judgment — the root of the evil inclination (<span class="lr">LM I:72</span>). Then He broke the evil inclination and joined mercy with judgment (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span>). Judgment from the initial contraction — necessary for revealing G-dliness — but immediately both contraction and light together (= judgment and mercy) produce the world. Excessive light → the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>'s hold (bad thoughts, heresy). Adam should have repaired all through one <span class="h">mitzvah</span> (not to eat, not to gaze, not to flaw thought, to engage in prayer = <span class="h">Malchus</span>). He failed — the Serpent's counsel (<span class="v">"you shall be like G-d"</span> = excessive light). Hashem called to him toward evening (<span class="h">Minchah</span> time): <span class="v">"where are you?"</span> (= <span class="v">"how did she sit"</span> = contraction = <span class="h">ayeh</span>). He was aroused to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: the essential — to begin anew each moment.</div>

<div class="p">All this is contained in Rabbainu's holy speech: <span class="mx">"G-d is great — we know nothing at all."</span> "Great is Hashem" = <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> illuminating the heart. "We know nothing" = tikun of the contraction. "Such things happen in the world — we know nothing at all" = the qualities/worlds drawn daily into the contraction. One must always return to not-knowing. <span class="mx">"Even your affairs..."</span> — <span class="mx">"but there is a matter..."</span> — <span class="mx">"for we know nothing at all."</span> The tikun: through crying and prayer. Even if done thousands of times. The holy mouth that forbade us all prohibitions itself said: all can be repaired through the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who reached the ultimate knowledge of not-knowing. <span class="v">"Do not be overly wicked, do not be overly wise"</span> — <span class="v">"His greatness is unfathomable."</span></div>

<div class="p">Three prayers: <span class="h">Arvis</span> = contraction; <span class="h">Shacharis</span> = drawing light. Both have <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> (= <span class="h">mochin</span>: even in contraction, wondrous <span class="h">mochin</span> exist — the <span class="h">Malbush</span> etc.). Each day: new creation; between days: contraction (= <span class="h">bain hashmashos</span> — a blink, this enters and this exits). From midday: <span class="h">Minchah</span> (no <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> — only the <span class="h">reshimu</span> of morning <span class="h">mochin</span> remains). One must be exceedingly careful with this prayer: there the secret of contraction and light together (= <span class="v">"Hashem Hu HaElokim"</span> twice, which Israel declared through Eliyahu). = <span class="h">Matan Torah</span> (<span class="v">"lest they break through"</span> — twice) → <span class="v">"I am Hashem your G-d"</span> and the Ten Commandments (= ten types of melody = <span class="h">shir pashut</span> etc.). All this can only be grasped "according to what each one estimates in his heart" (= melody — hinting to the heart). <span class="v">"I recall my song at night"</span>; <span class="v">"evening, morning, and noon I converse and moan"</span>; <span class="v">"woe to us, the day turns, the shadows stretch"</span>; <span class="v">"let my prayer stand as incense before You, the lifting of my palms as an evening offering."</span></div>

<div class="p">R' Shimon bar Yochai came right after the destruction — his tikun = <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> (the last House; through it, <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> will be repaired). He revealed the Idra Zuta only at his departure (after the Idra Rabba). Now all tikun through the departed <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s graves — the Ari"zal, the Ba'al Shem Tov, and onward. <span class="h">Mashiach</span> was born on Tisha B'Av (= the moon's fullness begins at maximum diminishment = <span class="h">Rosh Chodesh</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>). The essential: revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>) through service in this world. <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span> = <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span> (= <span class="h">Binah</span>). After departure: the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are <span class="mx">"found even more"</span> in this world — drawing the contractions from <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span> to this world, revealing G-dliness even to the most distant. Then: the Third <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> complete. The essential: through <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span> (gathering all Jewish souls). = Tikun of the <span class="h">Shekalim</span> (§14). R' Akiva (at the time of the destruction) — through his death al <span class="h">kiddush Hashem</span> (Ten Martyrs). Moshe's burial unknown (due to the sin of <span class="h">Shittim</span>). Now: tikun through the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s revealed graves — especially on <span class="h">Erev Rosh HaShanah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The essential: accepting His <span class="h">Malchus</span> voluntarily through free will (= <span class="mx">"they accepted His <i>Malchus</i> willingly"</span>; = <span class="v">"a woman cannot be sanctified except by her consent"</span>). The completion through <span class="h">Mashiach</span> — revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> through Torah <span class="h">chiddushim</span> (<span class="mx">"the first exile... the last exile, through your reed [pen]"</span>). Then: <span class="v">"Hashem shall be King over all the earth — on that day Hashem shall be One and His Name One"</span> (= <span class="h">yichuda ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">tata'ah</span>). And: <span class="v">"kings shall be your foster-fathers... they shall bow face to earth."</span> = <span class="h">Shemittah</span> and <span class="h">Yovel</span> (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> and <span class="h">ila'ah</span>); <span class="h">Yovel</span>'s holiness is on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> (<span class="v">"on <i>Yom Kippur</i> you shall pass the <i>shofar</i>"</span>). In the Zohar: <span class="h">Shemittah</span> and <span class="h">Yovel</span> are in the secret of <span class="h">Hai</span> (= <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span>). And: <span class="v">"you said to Hashem: <i>Adni</i>"</span> (= both houses united). <span class="v">"My good is not upon You"</span> — but <span class="v">"to the holy ones in the earth"</span>: through them all ascends. = <span class="h">Bereishis</span> = <span class="h">Bais Raishis</span> = <span class="h">Rosh Bais</span> — both encompassed together.</div>

<div class="cn">✦ End of Tefilas Arvis (on LM I:49), §§ 82–96 ✦<br>Completing the entire <i>LaShemesh</i> cycle:<br>Minchah H7 (§§1-81) + Arvis (§§82-96) = 96 sections<br><br><i>Na Nach Nachma Nachman May'Uman</i></div>

